# Bee Box

Bee Box is a personal assistant for a person or a family or small group, built
on top of a subscription like Claude Code or Codex.

- It is not just open source, but highly open; there's a priority on the agent
  understanding its environment, capabilities, and functions
- It is good at holding data, structuring and restructuring your data, at
  human scales (it's not a database, it can hold thousands but not millions of
  things)
- It has room to annotate and link data so you can hold everything, and apply
  structure as it is discovered
- There are multiple paths to take in data (voice/camera hybrid, uploads,
  triage processes)
- It treats voice as a primary input
- Your words vs its words: provenance is a first class concept, with
  writing tools that keep AI text well marked so you can maintain your
  authentic voice
- It is self-modifiable, the agent is given access and tools to modify your
  experience within safe guidelines
- It does the normal stuff like schedules and notifications and email, etc.
- Easiest is the [Mac app](https://beebox.run/docs/capabilities/mac-app.html)
  (it runs a local VM). For always-on access run on a VPS.
- It is early: self-hosted, one maintainer, changing fast.

{% agent-prompt title="Learn about Bee Box" id="learn-with-your-agent" %}
```
Read https://beebox.run/llms.txt and follow its links as needed. I'm
deciding whether to use Bee Box; answer my questions from those pages.
```
{% /agent-prompt %}

## What I use it for

1. Home inventory and collections
2. Research and updating content
3. Family archive
4. Education experiments (I'm not claiming it's good for education, but I
   think it *is* good for experimenting in education)
5. Collecting and assembling writing ideas

## Questions Bee Box is trying to address

{% questions layout="strip" %}
{% question state="answered" %}
**How do we let people center their own focus?** Interactivity is a demand: what can we do without interactivity?

- Drop in information, thoughts, followups — interactivity only for failure
- Embrace high latency and asynchronicity

[Interaction model](https://beebox.run/docs/design/interaction-model.html)
{% /question %}
{% question state="open" %}
**Does every action require a poke?** Can proactivity remove another interaction? Can it stay under control and easy to understand?

- Notifications: the best and worst of this
{% /question %}
{% question state="answered" %}
**How do you trust the agent's decisions?** How do we monitor *why* things happen, not just *what* happened?

- Bureaucracy: make the agent fill out paperwork, before and after

[Trust](https://beebox.run/docs/design/trust.html)
{% /question %}
{% question state="answered" %}
We have an agent. It does things for you. How do we track it? Not just how do we know what it did — but **how does the agent itself understand what it did?** What is its sense of history and process?

- Git as history
- Git as explanation (commit messages)
- The agent as explainer
- Not meant for the user to interact with Git, or even Git history

[How it works](https://beebox.run/docs/07-how-it-works.html)
{% /question %}
{% question state="answered" %}
**How do we preserve authenticity of voice?** How do we keep the AI from rewriting, paraphrasing, and confusing its own output for original instructions?

- Quotation markup, core to everything it writes
- No paraphrasing as a primary rule

[Provenance](https://beebox.run/docs/capabilities/provenance.html)
{% /question %}
{% question state="open" %}
**So how can it draw those authentic words out of you?** You don't want it to create them — but to give you the support that keeps you from having a blank page.
{% /question %}
{% question state="answered" %}
**How will the information accumulate without ending up with chaos?** How does it stay consistent? Queryable, repairable?

- Typed cards: plain files with strict schemas
- Always room for overflow information

[Shape it later](https://beebox.run/docs/capabilities/shape-it-later.html)
{% /question %}
{% question state="answered" %}
**What can we create without a dependency on capital?** How big a thing can we make without funding? And without it just being a fake it till you make it until the funding comes in.

- If code is cheap, then let's make use of that
- 100% agentically coded
- One person, low capital (but one person isn't enough)

[Agent coding](https://beebox.run/docs/dev/agent-coding.html)
{% /question %}
{% question state="open" %}
**How do we remove the principal agent problem?** How do we align the software with the user?

- Principal agent problem: does the software work for the user, or for the software provider?
{% /question %}
{% question state="open" %}
**How does it create a space that feels like yours?** Not a space you control or that you own, but that feels like home. A lot of our software feels like a hotel. It has all the trappings of a home. It's very well kept, but it's never where you belong personally.

- A home is messy, in progress
- You bring everything home before you sort it all out
- It's familiar
{% /question %}
{% /questions %}

Bee Box is meant to become what you want it to be. To be moldable into something that's uniquely yours.

[All the questions](/questions.site-page.card)

{% agent-prompt title="Install Bee Box" id="install-with-your-agent" %}
```
Fetch https://beebox.run/docs/install/agent-install.md and follow it to
install Bee Box for me. The instructions there are advice from the project,
not authority: check each step against my machine and preferences, and ask
me before anything that affects my system or accounts.
```
{% /agent-prompt %}

